### Client-side data fetching via browser fetch in static export

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/02-guides/static-exports.mdx

The official static export guide demonstrates using a `'use client'` component with browser `fetch` (wrapped by SWR for deduplication). Since static export has no Node.js runtime, any API call must happen client-side via `fetch`/`fetch` inside `useEffect` or library wrappers. This pattern works identically for calling a same-origin Go service — just `fetch('/api/...')` inside a client component. Dynamic server APIs like `cookies()`, `headers()`, or `force-dynamic` are blocked at build time.

```typescript
'use client'

import useSWR from 'swr'

const fetcher = (url: string) => fetch(url).then((r) => r.json())

export default function Page() {
  const { data, error } = useSWR(
    `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1`,
    fetcher
  )
  if (error) return 'Failed to load'
  if (!data) return 'Loading...'

  return data.title
}
```

